// A contract decorates a primitive WITHIN THE SCOPE OF THAT CONTRACT — the method is not part of the
// primitive's own API. Without this rule, any package declaring `type intrinsic <int32> implements
// Weighable { … }` would put `.weight()` on every `int32` in the program, including in code that has
// never heard of that package.
//
// Both legal spellings are shown in tests/comparable.kama: a bound, and a contract value. A user type
// that declares `implements C` in its OWN body is untouched by this — its methods are its own.
fn int32 main() {
    int32 n = 42;
    return cast<int32>(n.hash());   // `hash` is Hashable's, not int32's
}
